**The Myth of the Two Rivers**

In ancient times, two rivers flowed from separate mountains—the River of Silver and the River of Gold. Each nourished its own valley, but neither valley prospered fully, for Silver Valley had fertile soil but little sunlight, while Gold Valley had abundant sun but poor earth.

The River Spirit of Silver approached the River Spirit of Gold with a proposal: "Let us share our waters. I will send my fertile waters to your valley, and you send your sunlit waters to mine."

Gold hesitated. "If I send my waters away, what if you withhold yours? My valley will suffer."

Silver replied, "Then we both remain incomplete. But if we trust, both valleys will flourish threefold."

Gold agreed and sent half her waters flowing toward Silver Valley. True to her word, Silver sent her waters in return. Where the rivers met, a third valley emerged—the Valley of Bronze—more abundant than either valley alone, where neither river could distinguish her waters from her sister's.

The people learned: what we send forth multiplied returns to us, but only when trust flows both ways. The one who gives first bears the greater risk, yet creates the possibility for all to thrive.
